<p>Cuban President Miguel Díaz-Canel said at a rally in Havana on April 16 that Cuba does not want military aggression from the United States but is prepared to fight if necessary. He spoke during commemorations marking the 65th anniversary of the declaration of the Cuban Revolution’s socialist character and framed the moment as a period of serious threat, after President Trump said earlier this week that his administration could turn its attention to Cuba after the war in Iran ends. The remarks add to months of rising pressure between Washington and Havana, even as the sources indicate both sides have held talks aimed at de-escalation that have made little visible progress. Cuban officials paired their warning with a stated preference for dialogue, underscoring that the immediate significance of the speech lies less in a policy change than in how openly both the threat perception and Cuba’s readiness were expressed in public during a symbolic national anniversary.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/JtXYvRVlOrDw0aUjwmE4QzUhp7mfvwpxSWuC2yjLJLdekuiHtNdBjFKBV92E6YqMIdyrIvU8joCK8b0pv691SqpDQ8ccMlPmvPr-GHsTrp6c' alt='Cuban President Miguel Diaz-Canel speaks during a ceremony marking the 65th anniversary of the declaration of the socialist character of the Cuban Revolution in Havana on April 16, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Threat framing: Dawn reported that Havana has been bracing after repeated Trump warnings that Cuba is “next” following the fall of Venezuela’s Nicolás Maduro and the war in Iran.</li><li>Bay of Pigs force: The 1961 invasion involved about 1,400 anti-Castro Cuban exiles trained and financed by the CIA, according to Dawn’s account of the anniversary marked in Havana.</li><li>Family channel: Mariela Castro said 94-year-old Raúl Castro is indirectly involved in the current contacts with Washington, and Dawn reported that his grandson, Colonel Raúl Rodríguez Castro, is among the negotiators.</li></ul><blockquote>We don’t want that (confrontation) but it is our duty to be ready to avoid it, and if it were unavoidable, to win it - Miguel Díaz-Canel</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Miguel Díaz-Canel: He presented military preparedness as a form of deterrence, saying Cuba’s duty is to be ready in order to avoid confrontation if possible and to prevail only if it becomes unavoidable. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1992366/cuba-ready-for-possible-us-attack-president'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Mariela Castro: She drew Havana’s negotiating red line by saying dialogue with Washington cannot come at the price of putting Cuba’s political system up for debate. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1992366/cuba-ready-for-possible-us-attack-president'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/cuba-will-be-ready-possible-us-attack-trump-threats/'>Cuban leader says country will &quot;be ready&quot; for possible U.S. attack</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/americas/article/3350396/cubas-president-says-island-does-not-wish-us-aggression-ready-fight'>Cuba’s president says island does not wish for US aggression, but ready to fight</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/cubas-president-says-island-does-not-wish-for-u-s-aggression-but-ready-to-fight-if-necessary'>Cuba&#x27;s president says island does not wish for U.S. aggression but ready to fight if necessary</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/cubas-president-island-us-aggression-ready-fight-needed-132108982'>Cuba&#x27;s president says island does not wish for US aggression but ready to fight if needed</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/cuban-president-vows-defense-of-island-if-us-invades/a-76815996'>Cuban president vows defense of island if US invades</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1992366/cuba-ready-for-possible-us-attack-president'>Cuba ‘ready’ for possible US attack: president</a> - dawn.com</li></ul>